"hadiths[30]{hadithnumber,text}:"
184101,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Abu'l-Hubab Said ibn Yasar that the Messenger of Allah
184102,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Abu'l-Hubab Said ibn Yasar that the Messenger of Allah
184201,Malik related to me that Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha heard Anas ibn Malik say
184202,Malik related to me that Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha heard Anas ibn Malik say
184301,Malik related to me from Zayd ibn Aslam that the Messenger of Allah
184302,Malik related to me from Zayd ibn Aslam that the Messenger of Allah
184401,"Malik related to me from Zayd ibn Aslam from Amr ibn Muadh al- Ashali al-Ansari that his grandmother said, ""The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, 'O trusting women! Let none of you despise giving to her neighbour even if it is only a roasted sheep's trotter"
184402,"Malik related to me from Zayd ibn Aslam from Amr ibn Muadh al- Ashali al-Ansari that his grandmother said, ""The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, 'O trusting women! Let none of you despise giving to her neighbour even if it is only a roasted sheep's trotter"
184501,Yahya related to me from Malik that he heard that a beggar asked A'isha
184502,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said that death came to a man in the time of the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace. A man said, ""He was fortunate,"" as he had died without being tried by illness. The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, ""Alas for you, what will let you know that if Allah had tried him with illness, He would have wiped out his wrong actions."""""""
184601,"Malik informed me from Yazid ibn Khusayfah that 'Amr ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ka'b al-Salmi informed him that Nafi' ibn Jubayr informed him from 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. 'Uthman said: I had a pain that was almost destroying me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Place your hand on it seven times and say: \I seek refuge in the might of Allah and His power from the evil of what I find.\"" He said: I said that"","" and Allah removed what was with me"","" and I have continued to command my family and others to do so."""""""
184602,"Al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman heard him say: Charity did not decrease wealth, and Allah did not increase a servant with forgiveness except in honor, and a servant did not humble himself except that Allah raised him. Malik said: I do not know if this hadith is narrated from the Prophet ﷺ or not."
184701,Malik related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi from Abu Said al-Khudri that some people of the Ansar asked the Messenger of Allah
184702,Malik related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi from Abu Said al-Khudri that some people of the Ansar asked the Messenger of Allah
184801,Yahya related to me from Malik from Nafi from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah
184802,Yahya related to me from Malik from Nafi from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah
184901,Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam from Ata ibn Yasar that the Messenger of Allah
184902,Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam from Ata ibn Yasar that the Messenger of Allah
185001,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah
185002,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah
185101,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam from Ata ibn Yasar that a man from the Ansar of the tribe of Banu Haritha was herding a pregnant she-camel at Uhud. It was about to die, so he slaughtered it with a sharp stake. The Messenger of Allah was asked about that, and he said, ""There is no harm in it, eat it"
185102,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam from Ata ibn Yasar that a man from the Ansar of the tribe of Banu Haritha was herding a pregnant she-camel at Uhud. It was about to die, so he slaughtered it with a sharp stake. The Messenger of Allah was asked about that, and he said, ""There is no harm in it, eat it"
185201,Yahya related that Malik heard al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman say
185202,Yahya related that Malik heard al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman say
185301,Yahya related to me from Malik that he heard that the Messenger of Allah
185302,Yahya related to me from Malik that he heard that the Messenger of Allah
185401,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from his father that the Messenger of Allah
185402,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from his father that the Messenger of Allah
185501,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam that his father said, ""I went out with Umar ibn al-Khattab to Harra Waqim one night, and when we reached al-Waqim, he saw a fire and said, 'Aslam! I think these are some travellers who have been overtaken by the cold and the night. Let's go to them.' We hurried along until we reached them, and there was a woman with some children, and a cooking pot on a fire, and the children were crying. Umar said, 'Peace be upon you, people of the light,' for he disliked saying 'people of the fire.' Then he said, 'May I approach?' She said, 'Approach, or else leave us alone.' He approached and said, 'What is the matter with you?' She said, 'The cold and the night have distressed us.' He said, 'What is the matter with these children crying?' She said, 'They are hungry.' He said, 'What is in this cooking pot?' She said, 'It is water. I am quietening them with it until they sleep, and Allah will judge between me and Umar.' Umar said, 'May Allah have mercy on you! How would Umar know about you?' She said, 'Then why does he govern us and neglect our condition?'

Umar turned to me and said, 'Let's go.' We hurried off until we reached the store-house, and he took out a sack of flour and a container of fat, and said, 'Load this on me.' I said, 'O Amir al-Muminin, let me carry it for you.' He said, 'Will you carry it for me on the Day of Resurrection?' So I loaded it on him, and he hurried until he reached her. Then he put down the flour and put some of the fat into it, and himself set about cooking for her, blowing on the fire with his own mouth—and he had a large beard—until I saw the smoke coming out between his whiskers. He kept on until the food was cooked, then he said to her, 'Feed them,' and he did not leave until they had eaten their fill, and he left the rest of it with her.

Then he stood up, and I stood up with him, and the children began to play and laugh, and he sat listening to them for a while, then he said, 'You have made me glad, may Allah make you glad, O Aslam.' Then he said, 'Hunger made them do what you saw, and I could not leave until I saw with my own eyes their condition changed from what I had seen of it before, and I saw them laughing after I had seen them crying.'"""
185502,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam that his father said, ""I went out with Umar ibn al-Khattab to Harra Waqim one night, and when we came to Sirar, he saw a fire. He said, 'Aslam, I see some travellers who have been afflicted by the cold and the wind. Let's go to them.' We hastened towards it, and when we reached it, there was a woman who had lit a fire beneath a container and had some children with her who were crying. Umar said, 'Peace be upon you, people of the light' - he disliked to say 'people of the fire' - 'May I approach?' She said, 'Approach, or come near.' He approached and said, 'What is the matter with you?' She said, 'Cold and hardship.' He said, 'What is the matter with these children, that they are crying?' She said, 'They are hungry.' He said, 'What is in this container?' She said, 'Water, to quieten them until they sleep. Allah is the judge between me and Umar.' He said, 'May Allah have mercy on you. What do you know about Umar?' She said, 'He should be in charge of our affairs and unaware of our condition.' He turned to me and said, 'Let's go, Aslam.' So we hastened off until we reached the storehouse. He took out a sack of flour and a container of fat and said, 'Lift this onto me.' I said, 'I will carry it for you, Amir al-Muminin.' He said, 'Will you carry it for me on the Day of Rising?' So I lifted it onto him, and he hurried off with it until he came to her. He put it down and took out some of the flour and said to her, 'Pour some water for me.' She poured it for him, and he put in the flour and put in some of the fat, and stirred it himself, blowing beneath the container. I looked at his beard, and it was moving, until the food was cooked. Then he took some out and said, 'Bring me something.' She brought something, and he poured onto it, and said, 'Feed them, and I will wait for them.' They ate until they were satisfied, and he left what remained with them. Then he stood up, and I stood up with him, and we went off. He walked a little way, and then crouched down facing them for a time. I said, 'Amir al-Muminin, there is something else I wanted to mention to you.' He remained crouching down until he heard them laughing, and then he got up and came to me and said, 'Aslam, hunger kept them awake and made them cry, and I did not want to leave until I had seen what I have just seen.'"""
